   194	## 5. Fail-SAFE (`R-VEQ-4`) in the cache path
   195	
   196	`R-VEQ-4` — *any inability to run or verify yields `dense_disabled = true`, never
   197	skip-and-serve* — is HITL-signed and is **not** weakened. It is a guarantee about
   198	**accident** — corruption, truncation, a half-written file, a workspace that
   199	predates the cache — not about a hostile writer; §8 draws that line. In the cache
   200	path:
   201	
   202	- **an unreadable cache reads as a MISS, never as a pass.**
   203	  `probe_verification_is_cached` answers `false` for every failure mode: missing
   204	  `_fathomdb_open_state` table, absent row, non-TEXT value, garbled or truncated
   205	  value, a stale fingerprint, any SQL error.
   206	
   207	  A `true` means **the fingerprint inputs are unchanged since *some* engine
   208	  recorded a pass** — not "this engine verified this backend". The engine cannot
   209	  tell its own marker from an identical one written by anything else. §8 states
   210	  what that costs: not fixable by construction (§8.2), materially **cheaper** to
   211	  forge than the pre-slice route (§8.4), and bounded by the ruled residual (§8.5).
   212	  (`an_unreadable_cache_entry_falls_back_to_running_the_probe`,
   213	  `a_missing_cache_entry_falls_back_to_running_the_probe`);
   214	- **a failing verdict is never cached**, and every `Err` path of the check
   215	  **clears** any prior marker — so a workspace that could not be verified never
   216	  leaves one behind for a later open to match
   217	  (`a_divergence_on_a_rerun_still_disables_dense`);
   218	- **a divergence found on a re-run still disables dense.** The cache narrows *when*
   219	  the probe runs, never *what it concludes when it does*;
   220	- **a cache WRITE failure is swallowed** rather than turned into a refusal. The arm
   221	  has just been verified, so refusing dense because a marker could not be persisted
   222	  (read-only file, disk full) would be a false refusal — and the consequence of the
   223	  missing marker is that the next open re-runs the probe. More work, never less.
   224	
