   226	
   227	## 6. THE RESIDUAL — what is no longer caught on every open
   228	
   229	**Same-identity backend drift is no longer caught per-open.**
   230	
   231	The 0.8.18 probe exists to catch a backend that changed *without* changing the
   232	declared identity: candle CPU↔CUDA, a library or driver upgrade, a rebuilt ONNX
   233	runtime. None of those move any fingerprint input — the identity triple is
   234	unchanged (and would refuse the open if it were not), the mean is unchanged, the
   235	fixture is unchanged, the stored baseline is unchanged. So the cache **hits**, the
   236	drifted backend is never asked to embed anything, and the drift is not detected.
   237	
   238	It is detected only at the next open whose fingerprint *does* change — a rewritten
   239	`mean_vec`, a fixture edit, a mutated baseline, or a recipe-tag bump. By
   240	definition, a pure backend swap is not one of those.
   241	
   242	Before TC-68 this drift was caught at the very next open. **After TC-68 it may
   243	never be caught.** That is a real narrowing of
   244	`dev/adr/ADR-0.8.18-vector-equivalence-self-check.md`, and it is the ruled trade
   245	(`dev/plans/plan-0.8.20.md` §3 `R-20-VC`).
   246	
   247	It is asserted as an executable fact by
   248	`residual_same_identity_backend_drift_is_not_caught_on_a_cached_open`, so nobody
   249	can later read "preserving the guarantee" off the plan and assume it was preserved.
   250	
   251	**fix-2:** this residual is *also* what bounds the forged-marker finding — the same
   252	drifted backend is served off an honestly recorded marker, with no forgery involved.
   253	See §8.5.
   254	
   255	### 6.1 What IS still caught, on every open
   256	
   257	- **cross-vendor / identity drift** — refused at open by `check_embedder_profile`,
   258	  which is and remains the PRIMARY gate (`R-VEQ-5`: the probe is additive);
   259	- **a partial, non-contiguous, re-attributed, text-mismatched or wrong-length
   260	  stored baseline** — the fix-2 completeness validation runs before the cache gate;
   261	- **a mutated reference blob** — via the fingerprint (§2.4);
   262	- **a rewritten `mean_vec`** — via the fingerprint (§2.2);
   263	- **an un-runnable embedder** (panics, errors, wrong dimension) on any open that
   264	  actually runs the probe;
   265	- **the first-ever verification** of a workspace — population always runs the full
   266	  check before dense is enabled.
   267	
   268	### 6.2 Mitigations considered and rejected
   269	
   270	- **Re-run the probe every Nth open, or on a wall-clock TTL.** Rejected: it makes
   271	  open cost non-deterministic and unexplainable ("why was *this* open slow?"),
   272	  restores only a fraction of the coverage, and needs a durable counter/clock that
   273	  is itself a new correctness surface. It converts a clean "cached ⇒ 0" contract
   274	  into a probabilistic one. (fix-2 re-weighed the same mechanism from the *threat*
   275	  side, where it is the one candidate that buys real coverage — see §8.7. It is
   276	  still not taken here, and the trade is written down there for a later slice.)
   277	- **Cache only the P2 (L2) half and keep re-running P1.** Rejected: both halves
   278	  need the same 45 embeds, so this saves nothing.
   279	- **Sample k of the 45 probes per open.** Rejected: the D4 floors are calibrated on
   280	  the full 45-probe set (P1 floor is exact-zero over all of them), so a sampled run
   281	  is not the signed check, and `dense_disabled` would stop meaning what the ADR says
   282	  it means.
   283	- **Widen `EmbedderIdentity` (or the `Embedder` trait) with a runtime
   284	  backend/device descriptor** — e.g. `candle-cuda:driver=12.4` — and fold it into
   285	  the fingerprint. **This is the fix that would genuinely retire the residual**, and
   286	  it is deliberately NOT implemented here: it is public-surface work on a governed
   287	  type, it needs HITL sign-off, and it belongs with the batched governed-surface
   288	  decision the plan places after Slice 22. Recorded for that decision, not taken.
